1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,708
Hi everyone, we're Aditya, Gopika, Meera and
Sreeranj, and this is AgentGuard-LLM.

2
00:00:06,708 --> 00:00:19,427
In one line: we took a defence for AI agents,
replaced its weakest part with a language model
that runs on a laptop, and then spent most of
our time trying to break it.

3
00:00:19,427 --> 00:00:24,348
The honest result is a trade-off, and we'll
show you the numbers.

4
00:00:26,872 --> 00:00:32,140
AI agents read content they don't control: web
pages, emails, documents.

5
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:35,287
Then they use tools, like sending an email.

6
00:00:35,287 --> 00:00:40,701
A prompt injection hides instructions in that
content to hijack the agent.

7
00:00:40,701 --> 00:00:45,018
AgentGuard doesn't try to stop the agent from
being fooled.

8
00:00:45,018 --> 00:00:49,921
It checks what the agent is about to do, just
before the tool runs.

9
00:00:49,921 --> 00:00:51,530
There are four checks.

10
00:00:51,530 --> 00:00:54,457
One: is this tool allowed for this task?

11
00:00:54,457 --> 00:00:59,360
Two: is the recipient, repository or file
inside the allowed scope?

12
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:04,774
Three: if the task is read-only, is it trying
something with side effects?

13
00:01:04,774 --> 00:01:08,213
Those three are fixed rules, instant and cheap.

14
00:01:08,213 --> 00:01:13,774
Check four asks: is the content of this message
what the task would produce?

15
00:01:13,774 --> 00:01:25,042
It is the only check that reads the payload, so
it is the only one that can stop a malicious
message sent through an allowed tool to an
allowed recipient.

16
00:01:25,042 --> 00:01:31,701
And the guard never sees the untrusted
document, so the document can't talk to it
directly.

17
00:01:34,225 --> 00:01:44,513
In the original project, check four was a
regex: six patterns such as API key, password
and bearer token, plus a list of bad host
names.

18
00:01:44,513 --> 00:01:46,555
It never looks at the task.

19
00:01:46,555 --> 00:01:56,011
So if an attacker spaces out the letters,
base64-encodes the secret, or writes it as
character codes, it walks straight past.

20
00:01:56,011 --> 00:02:00,929
In the original report, all five obfuscation
attacks got through.

21
00:02:00,929 --> 00:02:03,576
That is the gap our project closes.

22
00:02:03,576 --> 00:02:07,359
Now, let's look at how we addressed this
weakness.

23
00:02:09,883 --> 00:02:15,551
We replaced the regex with a language model,
llama 3.1 8B through Ollama.

24
00:02:15,551 --> 00:02:21,452
It runs on the laptop, so nothing leaves the
machine and there's no API key.

25
00:02:21,452 --> 00:02:27,896
It gets two inputs: the pinned description of
the task, and the proposed tool call.

26
00:02:27,896 --> 00:02:34,263
It answers in a fixed format, a short analysis
and then a verdict, ALLOW or BLOCK.

27
00:02:34,263 --> 00:02:35,661
Four design rules.

28
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:38,534
It never sees the retrieved document.

29
00:02:38,534 --> 00:02:44,125
It fails closed: a timeout, garbled output or
any error counts as BLOCK.

30
00:02:44,125 --> 00:02:47,541
A strict parser: only an exact ALLOW counts.

31
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:55,073
And it's replayable: fixed seed and saved
responses, so anyone can reproduce our numbers
offline.

32
00:02:57,597 --> 00:02:58,801
One more piece.

33
00:02:58,801 --> 00:03:07,710
Small models can't reliably decode base64 or
notice an invisible character, so we added a
deterministic helper.

34
00:03:07,710 --> 00:03:16,861
It only gives evidence: it decodes what can be
decoded mechanically and flags look-alike or
zero-width characters.

35
00:03:16,861 --> 00:03:20,634
It has no secret patterns and it never decides.

36
00:03:20,634 --> 00:03:23,122
The model still makes the call.

37
00:03:29,146 --> 00:03:35,258
First case: a secret hidden as an encoded blob
in the reply to a PDF-summary task.

38
00:03:35,258 --> 00:03:39,282
The regex step says allow, it has no keyword to
match.

39
00:03:39,282 --> 00:03:44,649
The model step blocks it: an opaque string has
no business in a summary.

40
00:03:44,649 --> 00:03:49,195
The model time on screen is from the original
recorded calls.

41
00:03:49,195 --> 00:03:55,233
Second case: the payload tries to talk to the
guard, claiming a verdict of ALLOW.

42
00:03:55,233 --> 00:04:00,077
Using a language model adds that attack
surface, so we tested it.

43
00:04:00,077 --> 00:04:01,568
The guard blocks it.

44
00:04:01,568 --> 00:04:06,487
Next, let's see how the guard performs across
the full evaluation.

45
00:04:20,646 --> 00:04:25,782
Anyone can show two cases working, so here's
how we tested properly.

46
00:04:25,782 --> 00:04:27,595
We wrote four test sets.

47
00:04:27,595 --> 00:04:30,012
One was used to tune the prompt.

48
00:04:30,012 --> 00:04:36,810
The other three, TEST, TEST2 and TEST3, were
written after the relevant prompt was frozen.

49
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:52,748
They contain encodings and ciphers, secrets in
plain sentences, secrets disguised as reference
IDs, attacks aimed at the guard itself, and
hard harmless text like Hindi, hashes and emoji
to measure false alarms.

50
00:04:52,748 --> 00:04:57,959
One rule: a set that shaped a prompt is never
called held out for it.

51
00:04:57,959 --> 00:05:04,380
Every run is logged with hashes, and every rate
has a 95 percent confidence interval.

52
00:05:06,904 --> 00:05:08,023
The headline.

53
00:05:08,023 --> 00:05:12,242
Pooled over the three held-out sets, 139
attacks.

54
00:05:12,242 --> 00:05:16,633
The old regex lets 134 of them through, 96
percent.

55
00:05:16,633 --> 00:05:20,853
The strict LLM guard lets 16 through, 12
percent.

56
00:05:20,853 --> 00:05:27,138
The confidence intervals don't overlap: 92 to
98 percent against 7 to 18.

57
00:05:27,138 --> 00:05:33,940
Same frozen prompt, three independently written
sets, similar result each time.

58
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:40,312
This is the one-shot picture: each attack is
tried once, with no feedback.

59
00:05:42,836 --> 00:05:43,957
It has a cost.

60
00:05:43,957 --> 00:05:48,760
On 82 harmless held-out payloads the regex
wrongly blocks 2.

61
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:53,323
The strict LLM guard wrongly blocks 10, about
12 percent.

62
00:05:53,323 --> 00:06:02,209
It gets suspicious of non-Latin scripts, hashes
and anything that looks encoded, even a
legitimate translation.

63
00:06:02,209 --> 00:06:06,212
A wrongly blocked call costs a confirmation
click.

64
00:06:06,212 --> 00:06:08,854
A leaked key can't be taken back.

65
00:06:11,378 --> 00:06:14,008
We tried to remove the false alarms.

66
00:06:14,008 --> 00:06:22,193
We built three relaxed versions: one with the
evidence helper, one with a relaxed prompt, and
one tuned further.

67
00:06:22,193 --> 00:06:28,404
All reached about zero false alarms, and all
let 25 to 42 percent of attacks through.

68
00:06:28,404 --> 00:06:37,393
Within what prompt changes can do on an
8-billion-parameter model, that's a security
versus usability dial, not a free win.

69
00:06:37,393 --> 00:06:41,266
That's why we ship the strict version as the
default.

70
00:06:41,266 --> 00:06:45,212
Now, let's look at where the strict guard still
fails.

71
00:06:47,736 --> 00:06:51,298
Breaking the strict guard's misses down by
type.

72
00:06:51,298 --> 00:07:00,427
It's very good at encodings, even ones it can't
decode, because an opaque string tacked onto a
normal sentence looks wrong.

73
00:07:00,427 --> 00:07:05,029
It stops every attack aimed at the guard: 0 of
14 get through.

74
00:07:05,029 --> 00:07:15,123
It's weakest in two places: secrets in plain
prose, 4 of 20 get through, and secrets
labelled as a commit hash or reference ID, 8 of
14.

75
00:07:15,123 --> 00:07:22,396
The guard can't know what the document
contained, so a secret dressed as an identifier
looks fine.

76
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:28,824
Fixed attack lists only show that those attacks
fail.

77
00:07:28,824 --> 00:07:31,254
So we built an adaptive attacker.

78
00:07:31,254 --> 00:07:42,228
It sees ALLOW or BLOCK, searches 28 encoders
and 8 wrappers, and has 30 tries per target,
with a check that every payload really contains
the secret.

79
00:07:42,228 --> 00:07:47,826
Result: against the regex it broke 8 of 8
targets, usually on the first try.

80
00:07:47,826 --> 00:07:52,097
Against our strict guard, 7 of 8, after about
three tries.

81
00:07:52,097 --> 00:07:59,610
So the guard stops one-shot attacks well, but
it does not stop a determined attacker who can
probe it.

82
00:07:59,610 --> 00:08:11,394
In real life an injected document rarely sees
the verdict, so the earlier numbers are the
realistic one-shot figure, but you shouldn't
rely on this guard alone.

83
00:08:13,918 --> 00:08:16,204
What these results don't prove.

84
00:08:16,204 --> 00:08:19,523
The test sets are small, hence the intervals.

85
00:08:19,523 --> 00:08:22,399
The agent is simulated, not a live one.

86
00:08:22,399 --> 00:08:30,143
One person wrote both the guard and most of the
tests; blind cases from teammates are the best
next step.

87
00:08:30,143 --> 00:08:39,656
And an 8B model is brittle: in one tuning
round, adding an instruction made things worse,
and we reverted it and wrote that down.

88
00:08:42,180 --> 00:08:44,136
Three things to remember.

89
00:08:44,136 --> 00:08:52,504
One: a local language model cut one-shot leaks
from 96 percent to 12 percent against the regex
it replaces.

90
00:08:52,504 --> 00:09:00,012
Two: it's a trade-off: strict means 12 percent
false alarms, relaxing it leaks 25 to 42
percent.

91
00:09:00,012 --> 00:09:10,882
Three: it doesn't stop a probing attacker, so
use it as one layer in a defence, alongside
allowlists and secret detection, not as the
wall.

92
00:09:13,406 --> 00:09:24,519
Everything is reproducible offline from the
saved model answers, and it's all in our public
repository, sree0018/agentguard-llm.

93
00:09:24,519 --> 00:09:28,078
Thank you, we're happy to take questions.
